  "output": "## Reality check first\n\nBefore the findings: most of the surface area this kind of audit normally targets **doesn't exist as code yet**. Every function in `engine/pipeline/`, `engine/agent/graph.py`, `engine/connectors/{salesforce,warehouse,synthetic}.py`, and `engine/db.py` is a `raise NotImplementedError(...)` stub — that's Phases 2, 3, and 5 per `AGENTS.md`'s build order, not started. On the web side, only the landing/get-started/sign-up pages and the read-only `/sources` list are built; the conflict queue, runs list, and audit trail (Phase 4, items 12–15 — where real data-shape risk lives) aren't there either.\n\nSo rather than pad this with generic advice against code that doesn't exist, here's what I actually found in the implemented ~30 files, plus the concrete risks baked into the stubs given what `AGENTS.md` says those functions must do.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Real issue today: duplicate query instead of caching\n\n`web/lib/server/sources.ts:23-24` — `countSourcesBySide()` re-runs the full `listSources()` query (and re-parses every row through Zod) just to count kinds:\n\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();   // full second query if listSources() was already called this request\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nToday `listSources()` backs `/sources` and `countSourcesBySide()` backs `/get-started` — different routes, so no double-hit yet. But as soon as any screen needs both (e.g. a dashboard header showing counts next to the table), this becomes two round trips for the same rows. Next.js's automatic request memoization only covers `fetch()` — raw Drizzle/postgres calls need manual dedup. Fix now, cheaply:\n\n```ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\n\nexport const listSources = cache(async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n  return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n});\n\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources(); // now a single query per request, however many callers\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nWrap every function in `lib/server/*.ts` in `React.cache()` as they're added — it's free insurance against the exact pattern above compounding as `runs.ts`, `conflicts.ts`, `audit.ts` land.\n\n## 2. DB client — good pattern, one gap\n\n`web/lib/db/index.ts` already does the right thing for connection leaks: it caches the `postgres()` client on `globalThis` so Next's dev HMR reloads don't open a new pooler connection each time. No leak here. One gap: no `max` pool size is set on `postgres(url, { prepare: false })`. On serverless (Vercel), each concurrent invocation gets its own module instance, and with Supabase's pooler default limits, that adds up fast under load. Set it explicitly:\n\n```ts\nconst sql = globalForDb.canonSql ?? postgres(url, { prepare: false, max: 1 });\n```\n\n(`max: 1` is the standard recommendation for serverless + a transaction pooler — one connection per invocation, let the pooler fan out.)\n\n## 3. React re-renders — nothing to fix, worth confirming why\n\nAll 7 `\"use client\"` components (`nav-rail`, `pipeline-strip`, `landing-hero`, `get-started-hero`, `step-list`, `sources-empty-state`, `button`) are leaf-level and presentational, exactly per `AGENTS.md`'s rule (\"mark `use client` at the leaf, not the page\"). No client-side data fetching, no lifted state, no props causing cascade re-renders. `SourceTable` and the `/sources` page are Server Components, so the table doesn't re-render client-side at all — it's regenerated server-side per request. Nothing actionable here today.\n\nMinor, low-priority nit: `useReducedMotion()` is called independently in 6 separate components, each subscribing to its own `matchMedia` listener. Harmless at this scale (onboarding/landing renders once), not worth touching now — flagging only so it isn't rediscovered later as if it were new.\n\n## 4. Forward-looking: where N+1 *will* appear (Phase 4, not built yet)\n\nThe conflict queue (`AGENTS.md` calls this \"the signature UI element\") is the highest-risk spot in the whole project for N+1, because a `ConflictDiffRow` needs the conflict, its match, and *both* entities. Implemented naively — fetch conflicts, then loop fetching `match` by `matchId`, then loop fetching each `entity` — that's 1 + 2N queries for N conflicts. When you build `web/app/api/conflicts/route.ts`, do it as one relational query:\n\n```ts\nconst rows = await db.query.conflicts.findMany({\n  where: eq(conflicts.runId, runId),\n  with: {\n    match: {\n      with: { entityA: true, entityB: true },\n    },\n  },\n  orderBy: [desc(conflicts.severity)],\n});\n```\n\nRequires defining the `relations()` alongside the tables in `schema.ts` (not there yet — add it when this route is built).\n\n## 5. Forward-looking: engine writes must be batched, not row-by-row\n\n`engine/db.py`'s docstring already states the *reason* the engine writes directly to Postgres: \"a reconciliation run produces thousands of rows across `entities`, `matches`, `conflicts` and `resolutions`.\" When `write_entities`/`write_matches`/`write_conflicts` move past `NotImplementedError`, a naive per-row `INSERT` in a loop turns that into thousands of round trips per run. Use `psycopg`'s batch execution:\n\n```python\nfrom psycopg import Cursor\n\ndef write_entities(run_id: UUID, entities: list[CanonicalEntity]) -> None:\n    with conn.cursor() as cur:\n        cur.executemany(\n            \"INSERT INTO entities (run_id, source_id, external_id, entity_type, fields, \"\n            \"parent_external_id, last_modified_at) VALUES (%s, %s, %s, %s, %s, %s, %s)\",\n            [(run_id, e.source_id, e.external_id, ...) for e in entities],\n        )\n```\n\n(`executemany` batches; for very large runs, `copy` is faster still.)\n\n## 6. Forward-looking: FAISS search must be batched, not looped\n\n`engine/pipeline/blocking.py` — \"remaining records embedded, top-k neighbours retrieved via FAISS.\" FAISS's `index.search()` takes a matrix and returns top-k for every row in one call. Calling it once per unmatched entity in a Python loop is O(n) redundant native-call overhead for something designed to be vectorized:\n\n```python\n# Right: one batched call for all unmatched entities\ndistances, indices = index.search(unmatched_embeddings, TOP_K)  # shape (n, TOP_K)\n\n# Wrong: n separate calls\nfor emb in unmatched_embeddings:\n    d, i = index.search(emb.reshape(1, -1), TOP_K)\n```\n\n## 7. Forward-looking: prompt caching is mandated but not yet wired\n\n`engine/agent/prompts.py` already separates `build_ruleset_block` (identical every call) from `build_conflict_block` (per-conflict), which is the right split for Anthropic prompt caching. When `resolve()` is implemented, the cache breakpoint needs to actually be set — it doesn't happen automatically from the split alone:\n\n```python\nmessages = [{\n    \"role\": \"user\",\n    \"content\": [\n        {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": ruleset_block, \"cache_control\": {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}},\n        {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": conflict_block},\n    ],\n}]\n```\n\nWithout `cache_control` on the ruleset block specifically, every call re-sends and re-bills the full ruleset as fresh input tokens — this is the \"single largest cost lever\" the docstring calls out, so it's worth a regression test in `eval/harness.py` asserting cache hits once implemented.\n\n## 8. Forward-looking: BackgroundTasks will block the status endpoint it feeds\n\nThis is the one I'd flag most strongly. `engine/main.py`:\n\n```python\nbackground.add_task(execute_run, body.run_id)\n```\n\nFastAPI's `BackgroundTasks` runs the task **in the same process, on the same event loop**, after the response is sent — it is not a separate worker or process. `execute_run` is described as CPU-bound: embeddings, FAISS, pandas dataframe work over potentially thousands of records. If it's implemented as plain synchronous code, it will block the event loop for the run's full duration — including the `GET /runs/:runId/status` endpoint the console polls to show progress. The run would look frozen from the UI's perspective until the whole pipeline finishes.\n\nTwo real options when this gets implemented:\n- Run `execute_run` via `run_in_executor` on a thread pool (fine if the heavy parts release the GIL — numpy/FAISS mostly do), so `/status` stays responsive.\n- Or move it off FastAPI's process entirely (a simple `multiprocessing.Process`, or a real queue) if the accounts/embeddings scale grows past what a thread pool comfortably handles.\n\nWorth deciding before Phase 1's `execute_run` is written, since it's an architectural choice, not a later refactor.\n\n---\n\n**Priority if you want to act now:** #1 and #2 are small, real, do-today fixes. #4–#8 aren't bugs yet — they're the specific places the current stubs will become bugs if filled in the obvious way, so treat them as implementation notes for whoever writes those functions next (Phase 2/3/5 per the build order).\n",
